 
          <p>My latest post on the difficulties of <a href="/post/2026/07/creating-life-is-giving-life/">young parenthood</a> has loosened something in the back of my mind. In a few others&rsquo; too; thank you so much for your encouraging messages! These small replies might not seem like much to you, but they are to me. So again, thank you.</p>
<p>I&rsquo;d like to take a shot at solving the problems that surfaced. I&rsquo;m playing <em>The Long Game</em> now&mdash;I should enjoy the daily small wins, knowing that it&rsquo;ll be an exhausting ultramarathon, not a short sprint. I never fully realise this but a friend of mine might be right: I usually overly focus on the negatives instead of cherishing what I already have.</p>
<p>So how to turn that around? Perhaps I should simply repeat an experiment from 2013. In one of the first blog posts ever to appear on this site, I explain how I <a href="/post/2013/08/can-i-haz-happy-thoughts/">end my days with happy thoughts</a>. I never thought I&rsquo;d cross-reference to something more than 10 years old but there it is. The idea is simple: instead of letting frustration roar at full speed inside, try to shift your attention to small wins. Tiny little things that unlocked a smile are enough. A <em>daddy, look!</em> perhaps.</p>
<p>This is what I came up with in my journal:</p>
<p>
<figure><a href="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/07/i-guess-im-playing-the-long-game-now/happythoughts.jpg" class="lbox">
		<img src="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/07/i-guess-im-playing-the-long-game-now/happythoughts.jpg" loading="lazy" title="A &#39;small wins&#39; logbook in my journal." data-pagefind-index-attrs="title">
	</a>
	
		<figcaption>A &#39;small wins&#39; logbook in my journal.</figcaption></figure>
</p>
<p>The &ldquo;small wins&rdquo; page spread should cover the entire month and is divided into four columns: (1) day indication, (2) small win description/remarks, (3) mood, and (4) physique. It should be dead-easy to fill in otherwise I won&rsquo;t be doing it, hence the Studio Ghibli stamps. Totoro happy? Me happy. Totoro OK? Me so-so. Totoro with umbrella? Me expecting emotional rainfall. I have yet to stamp a happy Totoro in there but the point is mostly to what you&rsquo;d call &ldquo;get a grip&rdquo;.</p>
<p>For that reason, I don&rsquo;t want to write anything neutral or negative in the second column. I&rsquo;ll be needing a fine nib to squeeze everything in there. Maybe I should ignore the &ldquo;remarks&rdquo; title altogether. I wrote &ldquo;TIRED&rdquo; next to the first umbrella, but should I?</p>
<p>A considerate reader told me they take more pictures of their kids just running around in and around the house to look back on fondly afterwards. That&rsquo;s sound advice. We should do that more often and forget about it in the many hectic moments. I re-read three journals from a few years ago today and the pasted in photos (mostly from the <a href="/post/2021/09/hp-sprocket-mini-printer/">pocket printer</a>) always make me smile. Our daughter found the photos of the baby funny: &ldquo;hey look, that&rsquo;s mom and little brother!&rdquo; &ldquo;No it&rsquo;s not, it&rsquo;s you when you were a little baby!&rdquo; &ldquo;oh wow! but now I already am high up in the sky!&rdquo;. Note to self: order new pocket printer sheets.</p>
<p>The Long Game means leaving short bursts of dopamine behind. And yet I find myself buying fountain pens&mdash;the last purchase was three years ago&mdash;or scrolling through BoardGameGeek&rsquo;s The Hotness list to see what else I can pick up after I sold a few ones just lying around on the shelf. I often try to escape into my laptop or book or whatever when things get heated with the kids. I notice I grab my smartphone during breakfast while I never did that before. The Long Game means noticing, admitting, and trying to change behaviour. I guess stage one is as good as any place to start.</p>
<p>The youngest gave me a really hard time today. In August I&rsquo;ll be playing daddy-day-care for both of them and I already feel slight hints of panic rolling my way. The Long Game means acknowledging it&rsquo;s going to be a mess but we&rsquo;re going to be okay regardless. As my favourite philosopher Michel de Montaigne said: <em>the purpose of life is to live it.</em></p>
<p>A short sprint would mean this to be over in no-time. The look back into my previous journals already taught me that The Long Game is more than fast enough: one day you&rsquo;re writing &ldquo;hey today the first one said bye bye and walked off onto the street for the first time!&rdquo; and the next day you find yourself writing &ldquo;next month the youngest turns one what happened??&rdquo;</p>
<p>Small steps. Small wins.</p>
<p>I&rsquo;ll be writing them down.</p>


          
